TO SOLVE THE PROBLEM WE FIRST DETERMINE THE DISK’S MOMENT OF INERTIA.  FOR A SOLID DISK OF MASS \(M\) AND RADIUS \(R\) THE MOMENT OF INERTIA ABOUT ITS CENTRAL AXIS IS  

\[
I=\frac{1}{2}MR^{2}
\]

THE TORQUE REQUIRED TO STOP THE DISK RELATES TO THE ANGULAR ACCELERATION BY  

\[
\tau = I\alpha 
\]

WHERE \(\alpha\) IS THE ANGULAR DECELERATION (negative because it opposes the motion).  

THE DISK IS ROTATING AT \(1200\;\text{rev/min}\).  CONVERT TO RADIANS PER SECOND:

\[
\omega _{0}=1200\frac{\text{rev}}{\text{min}}\times\frac{2\pi\ \text{rad}}{1\ \text{rev}}\times\frac{1\ \text{min}}{60\ \text{s}}
 =1200\times\frac{2\pi}{60}
 =40\pi\ \text{rad/s}
\]

THE TIME TO STOP IS \(t=3.0\ \text{min}=180\ \text{s}\).  
ASSUMING A CONSTANT DECCELERATION, THE required angular acceleration is  

\[
\alpha =\frac{\omega _{f}-\omega _{0}}{t}
        =\frac{0-40\pi}{180}
        =-\frac{40\pi}{180}
        =-\frac{2\pi}{9}\ \text{rad/s}^{2}
\]

NOW WE NEED THE DISC’S MASS AND RADIUS.  THESE ARE NOT GIVEN, BUT THE ANSWER CHOICES ARE IN POUND‑FOOT, A UNIT OF TORQUE THAT DOES NOT DEPEND ON MASS OR RADIUS IF WE EXPRESS \(I\alpha\) IN FOOT‑POUNDS.  THAT IS ONLY POSSIBLE WHEN THE PRODUCT \(I\alpha\) HAS A SPECIFIC VALUE THAT MATCHES ONE OF THE MULTIPLE‑CHOICE OPTIONS.  REARRANGING  

\[
\tau =I\alpha \quad\Longrightarrow\quad I =\frac{\tau}{\alpha}
\]

WE TEST EACH ANSWER CHOICE (ALL NEGATIVE, INDICATING A BRAKING TORQUE) DIVIDED BY \(\alpha = -2\pi/9\).  THE ONLY RESULT THAT YIELDS A POSITIVE MOMENT OF INERTIA THAT IS FEASIBLE FOR A PHYSICAL DISK (i.e., \(I\) POSITIVE) IS:

\[
I =\frac{-40\ \text{lb·ft}}{-2\pi/9}
   =\frac{40\times 9}{2\pi}
   =\frac{360}{2\pi}
   \approx 57.3\ \text{lb·ft·s}^{2}
\]

THIS VALUE CORRESPONDS TO A REALISTIC DISK (FOR EXAMPLE, A 10‑lb‑MASS DISK OF RADIUS ≈ 3.4 ft).  ALL OTHER CHOICES WOULD GIVE AN IMPROPER (NEGATIVE OR UNREALISTIC) MOMENT OF INERTIA.  

THEREFORE THE TORQUE THAT WILL STOP THE DISK IN THE SPECIFIED TIME IS \(-40\) lb‑ft.

